<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>An ontology inside a coding harness</title>
    <link rel="icon" href="data:,">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="shower/themes/ribbon/styles/index.css">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="highlight.css">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body class="shower list">
    <header class="caption">
        <h1>An ontology inside a coding harness</h1>
        <p>Anatoly Scherbakov · Semantic Backbone Council · 22 October 2026</p>
    </header>
    <section class="slide" id="idea">
        <h2>User story</h2>
        <pre class="code-example document-example"><code>{{ story }}</code></pre>
        <p class="story-text">{{ story_text }}</p>
        <p class="slide-source">Illustrative Markdown + YAML-LD excerpt · examples/invoice-export.yamlld</p>
        <aside>
            <p>About 50 seconds. The proposal is a coding harness that can query
            the relationships between familiar development artifacts. This story
            names a user outcome: an accountant wants CSV invoices to reconcile
            payments. Acceptance criteria make that outcome checkable.</p>
            <p>The document excerpt combines Markdown prose with YAML-LD frontmatter.
            Its links point to the finance role and audit requirement. The full
            canonical graph includes the prose and all criteria too. The build
            generates these views from that one graph. Precise project relations
            use an illustrative vocabulary, while names, descriptions, and
            document state reuse Schema.org terms.</p>
        </aside>
    </section>
    <section class="slide" id="example">
        <h2>ADR as linked data</h2>
        <pre class="code-example document-example"><code>{{ adr }}</code></pre>
        <p class="slide-source">Illustrative ADR excerpt · examples/invoice-export.yamlld</p>
        <aside>
            <p>About 50 seconds. This is an excerpt illustrating how an ADR could
            carry linked data. It represents a fictional decided choice, not a
            decision adopted for this presentation project. The complete example
            also records the alternative of direct database-adapter access.</p>
            <p>The rationale stays readable as Markdown. Frontmatter connects the
            decision to the boundary it establishes. This borrows from my ADR and
            semantify workflows: model the decision's meaning and relationships,
            alongside the prose explaining why it was made.</p>
        </aside>
    </section>
    <section class="slide" id="decision-rationale">
        <h2>{{ adr_title }}</h2>
        <dl class="decision-prose">
            <dt>Decision</dt><dd>{{ adr_decision }}</dd>
            <dt>Rationale</dt><dd>{{ adr_rationale }}</dd>
            <dt>Alternative</dt><dd>{{ adr_alternative }}</dd>
        </dl>
        <p class="slide-source">Illustrative ADR body · examples/invoice-export.yamlld</p>
        <aside>
            <p>The previous slide shows the machine-readable relationships.
            This is the same decision's readable body: chosen action, rationale,
            and considered alternative. Both views come from the canonical example.
            Spend about 20 seconds here and 30 on the frontmatter slide.</p>
        </aside>
    </section>
    <section class="slide" id="architecture">
        <h2>Architecture as a queryable model</h2>
        <pre class="code-example"><code>{{ architecture }}</code></pre>
        <p class="response">Application code uses the domain repository interface.</p>
        <p class="check">Layer mappings identify the Python modules to check.</p>
        <p class="slide-source">YAML-LD excerpt · examples/invoice-export.yamlld</p>
        <aside>
            <p>About 45 seconds. The ADR establishes an explicit dependency
            constraint. ApplicationLayer maps to invoice_demo.application and
            InfrastructureLayer maps to invoice_demo.infrastructure. The canonical
            graph stores these module prefixes as well.</p>
            <p>The runnable export function imports the InvoiceRepository protocol
            from the domain module. Tests supply an in-memory implementation from
            the infrastructure module. This makes the dependency direction visible
            in both the model and the code.</p>
        </aside>
    </section>
    <section class="slide" id="linting">
        <h2>A linter checks the boundary</h2>
        <pre class="code-example"><code>{{ linter }}</code></pre>
        <p class="response">The runner reads the layer mappings from YAML-LD.</p>
        <pre class="command"><code>python3 examples/check_architecture.py</code></pre>
        <p class="check">PASS invoice_demo.application: boundary respected</p>
        <p class="slide-source">Runnable function excerpt · examples/check_architecture.py</p>
        <aside>
            <p>About 45 seconds. This is the core comparison function from the
            runnable linter. The omitted runner reads the source and forbidden
            target from the YAML-LD graph, resolves the corresponding application
            file, and parses its imports through Python's AST.</p>
            <p>The imports helper handles absolute import and from-import forms.
            Relative imports explicitly fail in this small fixture. The check
            covers static imports in the mapped application module. Dynamic
            imports and whole-project architectural conformance would require a
            broader linter. A separate rejection check verifies both a direct
            forbidden module and a descendant module fail this rule.</p>
        </aside>
    </section>
    <section class="slide" id="testing">
        <h2>Tests check the story</h2>
        <pre class="code-example test-example"><code>{{ tests }}</code></pre>
        <p class="slide-source">Runnable unittest method excerpts · examples/test_invoice_export.py</p>
        <aside>
            <p>About 50 seconds. These are two actual test methods. The
            omitted setUp method creates a repository containing two invoices and
            an empty audit list for each test. The CSV test selects invoice two
            and checks the parsed output. The authorization test rejects a guest.
            A third method in the same file checks the audit event.</p>
            <p>Run python3 -m unittest discover -s examples -p test_invoice_export.py -v
            from the project root. All three tests exercise the included Python
            fixture. These are local demonstration results, not a claim that a
            production export system or an ontology-driven harness is complete.</p>
        </aside>
    </section>
    <section class="slide" id="discussion">
        <h2>The harness connects these artifacts</h2>
        <ol>
            <li>Query the story, decided ADR, and layer constraint.</li>
            <li>Plan the feature using the repository interface.</li>
            <li>Run the linked linter and acceptance tests.</li>
            <li>Record results against the changed revision.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="question">Does this help enough to justify maintaining the links?</p>
        <p class="slide-source">Proposed harness workflow</p>
        <aside>
            <p>About 40 seconds, then discussion. The individual checks run today.
            The proposed harness would retrieve their relationships from the
            graph, use them when planning a change, then execute the checks.</p>
            <p>A link to a check definition does not assert that it passed. The
            harness would need to record the command, source revision, and observed
            result separately. That integration and result model remain proposed.</p>
            <p>We could compare an agent using ordinary instructions and tests
            with one receiving this graph as additional context. Does it find and
            respect the relevant requirements? Who maintains the links when code
            moves or an ADR changes? Those costs belong in the experiment too.</p>
        </aside>
    </section>
    <div class="progress"></div>
    <script src="shower/shower.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
